# LOG-084 evidence: ci/check_fcov_expectations.py's report parser and urg cross bins.
#
# PROVENANCE, so this file is reproducible rather than assertible:
#   build            dv/auto_dv/work/tb-infra/wit/w26, sources sha256 9ff74126059016cb (the build the landing claims; the unmutated control root of the reds shares this identity)
#   run              ecc_data_freq (the frequent data-injection entry), the last run into that build's database
#   database         <build>/vcs_simv.vdb, the build's own; the run directories hold none
#   urg invocation   urg -dir <build>/vcs_simv.vdb -format text -report <report dir>
#   report           52 covergroup Group lines in grpinfo.txt
#   checker          ci/check_fcov_expectations.py, sha256 first 16 282127c9994dd824, imported and its
#                    parse_groups_report called on the report directory, so the keys below are the parser's own
#                    output and not a reading of its source
#   produced         2026-09-04T12:23:24Z
#
# WHAT THE REPORT CONTAINS. urg names cross bins with their counts under a per-cross heading:
  grpinfo.txt:12453 | data_single_way0 483   1
  grpinfo.txt:12493 | data_beat0 459   1
  grpinfo.txt:12521 | single_frequent 904   1
#
# DEFECT 1, FALSE FAIL. The parser recognises only `Summary for Variable <cp>` and never `Summary for Cross <cr>`,
# so it emits no gen_ic_ecc_cg.<cross>.<bin> key at all and a manifest declaring a cross bin can never pass:
  keys the parser emitted for this group: 38
  of them keyed under a cross name: 0
#
# DEFECT 2, FALSE PASS, the serious one. The cross heading only clears the parser's in-bins flag and leaves the
# coverpoint name set, so every cross bin's count is attributed to the group's LAST coverpoint. cp_knob is last here,
# and these keys are cross bins wearing its name, with the counts urg gave the crosses:
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.data_double_way0                   0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.data_double_way1                   0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.data_hit                           0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.data_single_way0                   483
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.data_single_way1                   421
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.double_frequent                    0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.double_rare                        0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.single_frequent                    904
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.single_rare                        0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.tag_all                            0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.tag_double_way0                    0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.tag_double_way1                    0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.tag_single_way0                    0
  gen_ic_ecc_cg.cp_knob.tag_single_way1                    0
# So a DECLARED coverpoint bin reads as HIT whenever a cross bin of the same name is hit in that group.
#
# SCOPE, narrowed by LOG-084c after this file was first written: the flow's measured path does NOT invoke the checker
# on a raw urg report. gen_fcov derives a variable-form report in which each cross section becomes a variable section
# named by the component tuple, which is how manifests name cross bins, and calls the checker with --report-dir on
# that. So both defects above are confined to a DIRECT invocation on a raw report, which is what this file and the
# landing's local reds exercise. The flow's own self-test already drove both forms on real reports.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT MASK ANYTHING THE PART-1 MANIFEST DECLARES, both measured on the traceability CSV: this group's 22
# coverpoint bin names and its 16 cross bin names have an EMPTY intersection, and the coverpoint the mis-attribution
# lands on is cp_knob, which declares no bin in that manifest. The name `yes` is shared by three COVERPOINTS, which is
# not this hazard: the parser keys by coverpoint, so only a CROSS bin sharing a coverpoint bin's name can mask one.
